Public transportation systems can carry more people than individual cars, reducing the number of vehicles on the road and easing traffic congestion.
Buses and trains emit significantly less carbon dioxide per passenger compared to private vehicles, contributing to lower air pollution.
Reduced traffic congestion leads to lower fuel consumption and fewer emissions, promoting a more sustainable urban environment.
Studies show that cities with robust public transit systems experience lower levels of air pollution and better traffic flow compared to those reliant on private vehicles.
Investing in public transportation encourages fewer car trips, which in turn reduces urban sprawl and promotes more efficient land use.